  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Clitocybula oculus is a small, delicate mushroom belonging to the Porotheleaceae family, characterized by its diminutive fruiting bodies and subtle coloration. This fungus is found in New England and plays a role in the decomposition of woody debris in forest ecosystems.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Small Size",
      "description": "Fruiting bodies are typically very small, usually less than 1 cm in diameter, making them easy to overlook",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Gill Structure",
      "description": "Gills are decurrent to slightly decurrent and closely spaced, running down the stem with a characteristic appearance",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Color and Texture",
      "description": "Caps are pale to whitish or grayish with a delicate, fragile appearance and thin flesh",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Clitocybula oculus is found in moist, shaded forest environments of New England where it grows on decaying wood, leaf litter, and woody substrates.",
